TI  - A Comparative Study of CNN and Transformer Based Models for Dengue Prediction Using Remote Sensing Data
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever is a major public health problem in tropical and subtropical climates and accurate prediction of dengue outbreaks can lead to timely intervention and management of the disease. Recent advances in remote sensing technology have enabled researchers to collect satellite derived environmental data to predict dengue outbreaks through deep learning. This paper presents a comparative analysis of CNNs (Convolutional Neural Networks) and transformer based architecture deep learning models used to predict dengue fever using multispectral satellite imagery and historical data on reported dengue fever cases. We have used a CNN based (ConvLSTM) model to perform spatiotemporal modeling, along with the Vision Transformer (ViT) and Swin Transformer architectures that were also used for temporal modeling. All three models were built using a single integrated framework and evaluated using the same data preprocessing, temporal windowing, training protocol, and evaluation metrics. We have also researched the impact that various spatial representation learning models have on the satellite based dengue fever forecasting models in a controlled experimental setting. The research gives guidance for building systematic frameworks for comparing different deep learning architectures for epidemiological forecasting that use remote sensing methods.
