TI  - A Predictive Model for Dengue Hotspot Identification Using GIS and Exploratory Data Analysis for Barangay-Level Health Decision Making
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever is one of the public health challenges in the Philippines, especially in localized outbreaks at the barangay level. This study aims to identify dengue risks through the integration of geographic information systems (GIS) and exploratory data analysis (EDA). The predictive model utilized barangay-level dengue data from 2019 to 2024, comprising 456 records across 76 barangays, along with environmental and demographic factors such as rainfall, temperature, humidity, and proximity to water systems. To ensure data quality and prevent data leakage, a data preprocessing and feature engineering pipeline was implemented. Three classifiers were used in this study—Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and Support Vector Machine. Results show that Logistic Regression achieved the highest performance with 92.39% accuracy, 0.9603 AUC, and 0.9355 recall, outperforming the other models in identifying high-risk barangays. In conclusion, Logistic Regression is the most effective model for dengue risk classification, offering a strong balance between predictive performance and interpretability. The integration of Geographic Information Systems (GIS) further enhances the framework by enabling spatial visualization of dengue hotspots, supporting targeted public health interventions at the barangay level.
