TI  - AI Based Spatio-Temporal Modeling of Dengue in Senegal for Epidemic Detection and Prediction
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever remains a significant public health concern in regions such as Senegal, where the ability to accurately forecast cases is essential for providing an early warning system and planning interventions. This study evaluates hybrid models that combine LSTM, ARIMA and SSA in order to predict the number of dengue cases across multiple time horizons. The results demonstrate that the SSA-LSTM+ARIMA model delivers outstanding performance (mean absolute error (MAE) =0.06, root mean square error (RMSE) =0.09, R2=0.99), effectively decomposing temporal components and reducing noise. Using the the well know Latin America DengAI dataset, we compared statistical, machine learning and hybrid (ARIMA, LSTM and SSA-LSTM) approaches, adapting the best-performing models to local epidemiological and environmental conditions. When applied to Senegalese data, the model produced promising predictions, aided by data augmentation and the inclusion of simulated vegetation indices to capture local environmental trends. Although the small dataset and simulated variables limit generalization, this approach clearly demonstrates potential. These findings emphasize the importance of longer time series and real environmental measurements in improving robustness, paving the way for the development of operational tools that can anticipate outbreaks, identify high-risk areas and support public health interventions and similar setting.
