TI  - An Efficient Optimization Technique and Machine Learning Model for Dengue Fever Prediction
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever, transmitted primarily by Aedes mosquitoes, remains a major global public health concern, with a sharp rise in reported cases in recent years. As no specific antiviral treatment is currently available, effective prevention and early outbreak prediction are crucial for reducing disease impact. This study presents a data-driven dengue prediction framework based on environmental and climatic data. The proposed approach first applies robust data preprocessing techniques, including missing value imputation, outlier handling, and normalization, to ensure reliable model inputs. An adaptive genetic algorithm–based feature selection strategy is then employed to identify the most influential predictors for dengue incidence. Using the optimized feature set, multiple machine learning and deep learning regression models—namely Neural Networks (NN), Support Vector Regression (SVR), Random Forest (RF), Elastic Net (EN), and Gradient Boosting Regressor (GBR) are implemented and systematically evaluated. Model performance is assessed using standard regression metrics, including Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Mean Squared Error (MSE), and Coefficient of Determination (R2 score). Experimental results demonstrate that the genetic algorithm– optimized Gradient Boosting Regressor consistently outperforms the other models, achieving the lowest prediction error. The findings confirm the effectiveness of the proposed adaptive optimization and ensemble learning framework for accurate dengue forecasting and highlight its potential applicability in real-world disease surveillance systems.
