TI  - An Integrated, Technology-Driven System for Monitoring And Predicting Vector-Borne Diseases
PY  - 2026
AB  - Diseases such as dengue, malaria, and chikungunya pose public health risk in tropical regions and highly dense areas. Conventional surveillance methods are reactive, laborious, and do not offer real-time feedback, limiting early response. This study advances the idea of an affordable, all-encompassing system combining the Internet of Things (IoT) and Machine Learning (ML) for real-time detection and forecasting of the risk of vector-borne disease. Environmental parameters such as temperature, humidity, rainfall, and water level at potential mosquito breeding sites are sensed with IoTcapable sensor nodes and transmitted to a cloud server. Historical and real-time data are analyzed by the ML algorithms to generate outbreak risk levels in the near future. A web-enabled dashboard shows interactive risk maps and alerts at risk areas. The novel aspect of the system lies in the proactive, closed-loop Vector Control mechanism that determines vector presence in tandem with the forecasting of outbreak risk and initiates appropriate data-guided action on time for effective disease control.
