TI  - An Intelligent IoT-based Framework for Early Detection of Vector-Borne Diseases
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue, malaria, and chikungunya are examples of diseases spread by vectors, which continue to be a significant problem in population health because of changing climatic conditions, increasing urban growth, and more movement of people. The conventional surveillance systems of the manual and periodic level of monitoring usually cannot timely give alert of the outbreaks. The proposed project will involve a combined Internet of Things (IoT) and machine learning (ML) solution to real-time monitoring and prediction of the possibility of a vector-borne disease. The framework is based on the utilization of the following elements to measure temperature, humidity, rain-and-water stagnation, and geotagging with the help of NodeMCU microcontrollers and environmental sensors. Through Wi-Fi and MQTT, real time data are sent to the ThingSpeak cloud, processed and cleaned, normalized and extracted features, and stored in a cloud database. Random Forest and Decision Tree models are used to classify regions in low, medium, and high-risk areas. A web dashboard would include visualization and curve analysis in real time and automated notifications in order to assist in early detection and preventative actions directed towards public health.
