PY  - 2026
JO  - CITIES
AB  - Clarifying the driving mechanisms of infectious diseases is crucial for optimizing prevention and control strategies. This study integrated legally reported infectious disease surveillance data (2013-2022) and 20 multi-source indicators across 21 prefecture-level cities in Guangdong Province, constructing a progressive "XGBoost-SHAP-LiNGAM-DoWhy" framework. Results showed IR fluctuated upward while MR fluctuated downward, with pronounced spatial heterogeneity: IR was high in the Pearl River Delta (PRD) and Northern Guangdong; MR was high in Western and Northern Guangdong. SHAP analysis revealed that IR prediction was primarily associated with environmental-demographic factors (population density, precipitation, NDVI, PM2.5), while MR was dominated by ecological-medical factors (NDVI, doctors per capita). The COVID-19 period indicator contributed zero predictive importance to both outcomes. Causal inference confirmed PM2.5 as the sole significant causal driver of IR (ATE = +0.304, p = 0.002), while doctors (ATE = -0.335, p < 0.001) and GDP per capita (ATE = -0.279, p < 0.001) were the dual causal determinants of MR. CausalSHAP heterogeneity analysis further revealed that GDP per capita significantly modifies the effects of both PM2.5 and doctors, highlighting the moderating role of economic development. This study establishes the "environment-driven incidence, system-mediated mortality" framework and provides evidence-based targets for regionally differentiated prevention and control.
