TI  - Analyzing and Predicting Health Impacts of Environmental Changes on Communities
PY  - 2026
AB  - Climate change has become more influential on the health of people across the world by raising temperature fluctuations, reducing rainfall, and reducing the quality of the air. These environmental disturbances have especially facilitated the rapid transmission of vector-borne diseases such as dengue and malaria, especially in congested urban regions. In this paper, the Early Disease Alert System is an AI-based system, which operates with combined data on climate, pollution, and epidemiological data to predict disease outbreaks. To determine the risk of hotspots and the relationship between climate and disease, the system uses Prophet models to predict short-term climate and Random Forest regression to forecast disease-cases. The state-level disease data, air-quality measures, and monthly IMD climate data were run to generate city-level predictive data. The system also has a risk-scoring mechanism to give actionable alerts. The outcomes of experiments prove that this framework is an effective predictor of disease surges, and it is a reliable and scalable tool to consider climatic resilient planning of the population.
