TI  - AutoDengue-LK: An Autonomous, Dynamically Updating System for Dengue Surveillance and Forecasting in Colombo, Rathnapura, and Kandy Using Government Data
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever remains a critical public health challenge in Sri Lanka, where outbreaks continue to inflict significant morbidity and strain healthcare resources. Current surveillance systems rely primarily on passive, retrospective analysis, limiting the ability of authorities to implement timely, proactive interventions. To address this gap, this study presents AutoDengue-LK, an autonomous, dynamically updating surveillance and forecasting system calibrated for the Colombo, Rathnapura, and Kandy districts. Unlike static modeling approaches, this system integrates official epidemiological and meteorological data streams through a continuous, automated pipeline. Forecasting is achieved via a spatially adaptive hybrid framework that fuses statistical time series methods with machine learning techniques to capture complex, non-linear transmission dynamics. A key novelty of the system is its automated retraining capability, allowing models to self calibrate against evolving disease patterns. The system is operationalized through an interactive early-warning dashboard that generates district level risk indicators and automated alerts. Performance evaluation using walk forward validation demonstrates that AutoDengue-LK achieves superior predictive accuracy (lower RMSE and MAE) and earlier outbreak detection compared to conventional baseline models, offering a robust tool for evidence-based public health decision-making.
