AU  - K. B. Jayanthi
TI  - Climate-Driven Disease Dynamics: A Scoping Review of Machine Learning Applications in Predicting Vector-Borne, Tick-Borne, Airborne, and Waterborne Diseases
PY  - 2026
AB  - The increasing global incidence of vector-borne, airborne, waterborne, and tick-borne diseases is strongly influenced by climate variability, environmental factors, and human activity. This scoping review analyzed 34 studies published between 2019 and 2024 to assess the role of climate data in predicting and managing these diseases. The findings show that increasing temperatures, changing rainfall patterns, and extreme weather events significantly influence disease transmission by expanding vector habitats and altering pathogen survival. Vector-borne diseases such as dengue and malaria are strongly affected by temperature and humidity, while airborne diseases like influenza and tuberculosis are influenced by air pollution and humidity changes. Waterborne diseases, including cholera, are associated with flooding and contaminated water sources, and tick-borne diseases show geographic expansion due to warmer climates and longer activity seasons. Although climate-based forecasting models, IoT sensors, and remote sensing have improved early warning systems, gaps in regional surveillance data remain. The study highlights the need for interdisciplinary approaches combining climate science, machine learning, and public health to strengthen outbreak preparedness and prediction.
