TI  - Climate-Driven Habitat Suitability of Aedes Aegypti Mosquitoes Using GBIF and Worldclim Datasets
PY  - 2026
JO  - International Journal of Research and Innovation in Applied Science
AB  - Climate change is altering the environmental conditions that regulate the survival, establishment, and geographical distribution of Aedes aegypti, the principal vector of dengue, chikungunya, Zika, and yellow fever. This study assessed the current and future global climatic suitability of A. aegypti by integrating 7,519 occurrence records obtained from the Global Biodiversity Information Facility with historical and projected bioclimatic variables from WorldClim. Following coordinate validation, duplicate removal, geographical screening, and spatial filtering, 3,792 unique occurrence locations were retained before final thinning. Nineteen bioclimatic variables were initially evaluated, after which nine predictors were retained based on correlation analysis, variance inflation factors, ecological relevance, and future transferability. Maximum Entropy, Generalised Linear Models, Generalised Additive Models, Random Forest, Boosted Regression Trees, Multivariate Adaptive Regression Splines, and Support Vector Machines were calibrated using five-fold spatial block cross-validation and combined in a performance-weighted ensemble. Future suitability was projected for the 2050s and 2070s under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5 using five CMIP6 global climate models. The ensemble outperformed the individual models, with a mean testing AUC of 0.96, TSS of 0.87, continuous Boyce Index of 0.91, sensitivity of 0.90, specificity of 0.92, and Brier score of 0.07. Minimum temperature of the coldest month, temperature seasonality, annual precipitation, precipitation of the warmest quarter, and maximum temperature of the warmest month jointly accounted for 79.8% of model importance. Current suitable habitat covered approximately 19.94 million km², representing 14.8% of the analysed global terrestrial area, with major concentrations in South and Southeast Asia, Central America, the Caribbean, northern and central South America, equatorial sub-Saharan Africa, and northern Australia. By the 2070s, suitable habitat was projected to increase by 12.6% under SSP2-4.5 and 23.7% under SSP5-8.5. Expansion was strongest in southern and central Europe, East Asia, North America, southern South America, southern Africa, and tropical highlands, while contraction occurred in parts of the Amazon Basin, the Sahel, the Horn of Africa, the Arabian Peninsula, northern Australia, and other increasingly hot and arid tropical regions. Projection uncertainty was greatest near range margins and in regions where climate models disagreed on future precipitation. The findings provide a reproducible basis for identifying present and emerging surveillance priorities and for strengthening climate-sensitive vector monitoring, early-warning systems, and public health adaptation.
