TI  - Dengue Prediction Using Google Search Trends: A Machine Learning Approach
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue outbreaks have become a major threat to public health in Bangladesh with record numbers of cases reported in recent years. Traditional prediction models largely depend on clinical and environmental data. We explored the potential of Google Search Trends as a predictive feature for dengue case forecasting. Using search trends related to symptoms, test names and treatments, we trained machine learning models including XGBoost, CatBoost, Gradient Boosting, Random Forest, LightGBM and Linear Regression. Among these, XGBoost achieved the best performance with an R2 of 0.867, MAE of 303.39 and RMSE of 779.77 across the full rolling window evaluation. Lagged features were found to be the most influential in predicting dengue cases. In addition, we compared the proposed framework against SARIMAX and LSTM baselines, demonstrating that machine learning models consistently outperformed traditional time-series and deep learning approaches. The results suggest that combining real-time internet search behavior with epidemiological data can provide a rapid and practical warning system for public health authorities in Bangladesh.
