TI  - DisPred-Net: A Hybrid Deep Learning Model Integrating TCN and XGBoost for Seasonal Disease Forecasting in India
PY  - 2026
AB  - Accurate forecasting of seasonal disease outbreaks is critical for timely public health response in India’s diverse climatic and demographic landscape. This paper introduces DisPred-Net, a novel hybrid deep learning model that integrates Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN) with XGBoost to predict disease case trends using multi-source spatiotemporal data. The model is trained and evaluated on the EpiClim dataset, a comprehensive weekly district-level collection covering epidemic-prone diseases in India from 2009 to 2023, incorporating environmental (temperature, precipitation, LAI), epidemiological (cases, deaths), and geographic features. DisPred-Net first captures long-range temporal dependencies via TCN, then fuses this representation with structured features using XGBoost to model complex non-linear relationships. Experimental results demonstrate that DisPred-Net achieves superior performance with a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 3.12, Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 4.89, and a coefficient of determination (R2) of 0.927, outperforming baseline models such as LSTM (R2 = 0.872), Random Forest (R2 = 0.846), and SVR (R2 = 0.803). The model is also a predictive forecasting tool in real-time and long-term that is easy to interpret and uses the outcomes effectively to predict future outbreaks through 2030 therefore it is an effective and predictive tool in real-time and long term to make predictions about the future outbreaks.
