TI  - Federated LSTM-GAN Approach to Predicting Malaria and Dengue in Indonesia
PY  - 2026
AB  - Indonesia faces significant challenges from climate-sensitive vector-borne diseases (VBDs) like malaria and dengue fever. This research presents a federated LSTM-GAN model for early disease prediction, transitioning from reactive case reporting to proactive surveillance. Working with Indonesian partners using SISMAL surveillance data and BMKG meteorological services, we implemented federated training across 8 countries with 5 neural network architectures. Counter-intuitively, our simplest hybrid model achieved superior performance ($R^{2}=0.9999981$, SMAPE $=94.55 \%$) with only 27,000 parameters versus $350,000+$ for complex alternatives. The federated approach preserved data sovereignty while enabling cross-regional learning, supporting Indonesia's ClimateSmart health strategy and demonstrating practical feasibility for surveillance transformation.
