TI  - Hybrid Transformer–GRU Architecture for Time Series Forecasting of Dengue Incidence
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever remains a major public health challenge in tropical countries, including Indonesia, due to its increasing incidence and complex transmission dynamics. Accurate forecasting of dengue cases is important for supporting early warning systems and improving public health preparedness. However, conventional statistical approaches such as ARIMA often struggle to model nonlinear and seasonal temporal patterns in epidemiological time series data, resulting in limited forecasting performance. This study proposes a hybrid Transformer–GRU architecture for multivariate dengue incidence forecasting. The proposed model combines the Transformer’s attention-based capability for capturing global temporal dependencies with the sequential learning efficiency of the Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU). The dataset used in this study consists of 12,384 monthly multivariate dengue records collected from multiple districts and subdistricts in Bengkulu Province, Indonesia, covering the period from 2017 to 2024. Environmental and demographic variables, including temperature, humidity, rainfall, rainy days, and population density, were incorporated into the forecastingprocess. The proposed framework performs one-step-ahead monthly dengue forecasting using a chronological train–test split strategy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed Transformer–GRU model outperforms conventional statistical and standalone deep learning models across all evaluation metrics. The model achieved an MSE of 1.6895, RMSEof 1.2998, MAE of 0.7223, and R2 of 0.3232, indicating substantial improvement in predictive performance compared to the evaluated baseline models. Although the obtained R2 value remains moderate, the proposed hybrid architecture effectively captures overall temporal trends and seasonal fluctuations in dengue incidence data. These findings demonstrate the potential of hybrid attention-based and recurrent architectures for epidemiological time series forecasting and public health monitoring.
