PY  - 2026
AB  - Recent disease outbreaks have highlighted the urgent need for models capable of predicting future epidemic occurrences. In this work, we trained several machine learning models, including Support Vector Machine (SVM), CatBoost, Gradient Boost, and Logistic Regression, using historical district-wise data associated with disease outbreaks like malaria, dengue, cholera, and chikungunya. When these models are compared, CatBoost outperformed the others with an accuracy of 86.6%. We created an intuitive web-based platform integrated with a chatbot to guarantee accessibility and useful usability. In order to obtain map-based visualizations and probabilistic predictions showing areas where outbreaks are more likely to occur, users can enter parameters like year, month, and disease type. The suggested system is intended to serve as an early warning system which helps people to take preventive actions to reduce the effects of disease outbreaks.
