TI  - Prediction and Analysis of Bangladesh’s Recent Dengue Surge by Artificial Intelligence with LSTM Time Series Forecasting Method
PY  - 2026
AB  - A virus named dengue which has become pervasive nowadays due to mosquitoes and that consequently poses a parlous concern to public health in many tropical and subtropical areas. Since alarming outbreak of dengue in recent months, deadly aftermath is inevitable unless proper amelioration scheme is implemented. Early prescient steps and unimpeachable preventive actions can be a game changer to lessen the effects of dengue outbreaks. This research aims to develop a rigor predictive model for dengue occurrence using machine learning methods like K-Nearest Neighbors(KNN), Random Forest, Naïve Bayes, Support Vector Machine (SVM), Decision Tree, Logistic Regression, and particularly Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) neural networks, along with time series forecasting approaches. In Logistic Regression, 99.98% is the greatest predicted accuracy score with 100% precision, recall, and f1-score. And 90% to 95% accuracy is projected for the other classifiers. Both train and validation score accuracy is enhanced along with good time series forecasting by employing the LSTM approach. LSTM obtained the best 99% of accuracy. The suggested method aggregates historical dengue incidence data, meteorological data, and other relevant factors with a view to building an impeccable dataset appropriate for predictive modeling.. In essence, engineers, future researchers and the inhabitants from affected area all are benefitted from this model by having a wakeup call regarding the perilousness of this virus.
