TI  - Prediction of Vector Borne Diseases Using Machine Learning Models Based on Environmental and Clinical Data
PY  - 2026
AB  - Delayed detection and ineffective treatment are the main reasons why mosquito-borne illnesses like dengue fever and malaria have become a major obstacle to health advancement in tropical areas. In order to predict vector-borne diseases by examining environmental and clinical characteristics, the proposed work assesses five machine learning models: Support Vector Machine (SVM), K-Means clustering, Random Forest, Naive Bayes, and K-Nearest Neighbours (KNN). These models are trained and tested in the study using a labelled dataset from certain geographic locations. Oversampling techniques are used to guarantee a balanced class distribution in order to address the problem of imbalanced datasets. With an 89% prediction accuracy, the SVM model outperforms K-Means clustering (86%), Random Forest (67%), Naive Bayes (64%), and KNN (30%). Early vector-borne infection prediction is made easier by the SVM-based model's incorporation of clinical and environmental variables. The results of the trial demonstrate the strategy can save time and money by allowing medical professionals to take preventative action before crises occur. A thorough assessment of the models' performance is provided by a detailed examination of recall, precision, and F1-score.
