TI  - Predictive Modeling of Malaria and Dengue Outbreaks Using Multi-Modal Machine Learning and Climate-Health Data Integration
PY  - 2026
AB  - Malaria and dengue fever are still the main causes of illness in the tropics and subtropics, areas severely affected by these diseases, and their transmission has been greatly affected by climatic changes and the environment. Most traditional disease monitoring systems have delayed reports and are also limited in predicting situations. This article puts forward a holistic multimodal machine learning strategy that combines heterogeneous data sources such as past disease incidence, meteorological parameters, remote sensing data, and sociodemographic information for precise prediction of malaria and dengue outbreaks. Our method features a hybrid design that merges temporal models (LSTM, GRU) for sequential pattern recognition, ensemble methods (XGBoost, Random Forest) to deal with non-linear relationships, and attention mechanisms for feature importance weighting. The system is capable of processing climate variables (temperature, precipitation, humidity), vegetation indices (NDVI, LAI), and historical case data to predict outbreaks weekly at the district-level granularity. The exhaustive experiments carried out on real-world datasets from various Indian states testify to the framework’s power. It achieves 94.2% prediction accuracy and an F1-score of 0.927 for outbreak classification. The enacted system gives the public health department a tool in their hands to intervene before the situation gets out of control and also to allocate resources in the most efficient way, thus, reducing the disease burden through early warning capabilities.
