TI  - Temporal Random Forest to Predict Dengue Hotspots in 11 Districts of Bangladesh Using Meteorological Data
PY  - 2026
AB  - Dengue fever poses a significant and recurring public health threat in Bangladesh, with its transmission dynamics being heavily influenced by meteorological conditions. The effective prediction of dengue hotspots is critical for enabling timely public health interventions. This study presents a lightweight machine learning framework to predict monthly dengue patient counts, which is a key indicator for identifying potential hotspots. In this research, a Random Forest Regressor model is developed to train on historical meteorological data including rainfall, temperature, and humidity and dengue patient records from 2011 to 2021. This approach presents a temporal feature engineering strategy that structures the data to explicitly teach the model long-term seasonal patterns by using the same month from previous years as input features. On a hold-out test set for the year 2020, this model has achieved a notable $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ score of 0.335 and an accuracy of 96.97% for predictions within a 2 patient tolerance. The proposed model significantly has outperformed several alternative architectures, including those based on Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). The results demonstrate that this approach can serve as a valuable and reliable tool for public health authorities to proactively identify high-risk areas, facilitating efficient resource allocation and targeted vector control measures.
