TI  - Aedes albopictus Is Rapidly Invading Its Climatic Niche in France: Wider Implications for Biting Nuisance and Arbovirus Control in Western Europe
PY  - 2025
JO  - GLOBAL CHANGE BIOLOGY
AB  - The Asian tiger mosquito, Aedes albopictus, is a competent vector of arboviruses, such as dengue. After its introduction into southern Europe, this invasive species has been rapidly spreading as well as causing autochthonous cases of arboviral diseases. Both Ae. albopictus presence and potential to transmit arboviruses are facilitated at warm temperatures; hence, global warming is expected to affect their presence in temperate regions. We use a climate- and environmental-driven mechanistic modeling framework to investigate the impact of recent climate change on Ae. albopictus range expansion and its potential to transmit dengue in Western Europe. We simulate climatic suitability, adult density, and dengue transmission risk, which we compare with a large ensemble of entomological and epidemiological observations over the past 20 years. Most importantly, we analyze a novel spatiotemporal dataset of colonized municipalities in metropolitan France to estimate the spread rate of Ae. albopictus and compare it with model predictions. Lastly, we analyze the sensitivity of entomological and epidemiological risk to changes in temperature, rainfall, and human density. Distribution of simulated mosquito populations and dengue transmission risk satisfactorily match entomological and dengue observations for Western Europe (AUC = 0.90 and 0.75 respectively). While lowlands in southern Europe were already climatically suitable for hosting Ae. albopictus around 2010, Western France, together with large populated cities, such as London, Zagreb, and Vienna, has become suitable recently. Importantly, the accelerating colonization of Ae. albopictus in France may be approaching the limit of its theoretical climatic niche; future expansion will depend on the climate-driven enlargement of suitable areas. The area at risk of dengue transmission has recently expanded from the Mediterranean coasts over northern Spain and Western France. The sensitivity analysis suggests that climate change may expose medium-sized cities to the highest epidemiological risk; this finding is consistent with recently reported dengue outbreaks in Europe.Le moustique tigre, Aedes albopictus, est un vecteur d'arboviroses tels que la dengue et le chikungunya. Depuis son introduction dans le sud de l'Europe, cette esp & egrave;ce invasive s'est rapidement r & eacute;pandue, provoquant des cas autochtones d'arboviroses. Sa pr & eacute;sence et son potentiel de transmission de maladies sont facilit & eacute;s par des temp & eacute;ratures chaudes, sugg & eacute;rant que le r & eacute;chauffement climatique influence leur pr & eacute;sence dans les r & eacute;gions temp & eacute;r & eacute;es. Nous utilisons un cadre de mod & eacute;lisation m & eacute;caniste ax & eacute; sur le climat et l'environnement pour & eacute;tudier l'impact du changement climatique r & eacute;cent sur l'expansion de l'aire de r & eacute;partition d'Ae. albopictus et son potentiel de transmission de la dengue en Europe occidentale. Nous simulons un param & egrave;tre de favorabilit & eacute; climatique, la densit & eacute; des adultes et le risque de transmission de la dengue, que nous comparons & agrave; un vaste ensemble d'observations entomologiques et & eacute;pid & eacute;miologiques collect & eacute;es au cours des 20 derni & egrave;res ann & eacute;es. Surtout, nous analysons un nouveau jeu de donn & eacute;es spatio-temporelles de municipalit & eacute;s colonis & eacute;es en France m & eacute;tropolitaine pour estimer le taux de propagation d'Ae. albopictus et le comparer aux pr & eacute;dictions du mod & egrave;le. Enfin, nous & eacute;valuons la sensibilit & eacute; du risque entomologique et & eacute;pid & eacute;miologique aux variations de temp & eacute;rature, de pluviom & eacute;trie et de densit & eacute; humaine. Les simulations de la r & eacute;partition spatiale du moustique et du risque de transmission de la dengue correspondent de mani & egrave;re satisfaisante aux observations entomologiques et & eacute;pid & eacute;miologiques pour la dengue en Europe occidentale (AUC = 0.90 et 0.75 respectivement). Alors que les plaines de l'Europe m & eacute;ridionale & eacute;taient d & eacute;j & agrave; climatiquement favorables pour Ae. albopictus en 2010, l'ouest de la France, ainsi que des villes peupl & eacute;es telles que Londres, Zagreb et Vienne, le sont devenues plus r & eacute;cemment. Il est important de noter que la colonisation acc & eacute;l & eacute;r & eacute;e d'Ae. albopictus en France pourrait bient & ocirc;t approcher la limite de sa niche climatique th & eacute;orique; l'expansion future d & eacute;pendra de l'& eacute;largissement des zones favorables induites par le changement climatique. La zone & agrave; risque de transmission de la dengue s'est r & eacute;cemment & eacute;tendue des r & eacute;gions littorales m & eacute;diterran & eacute;ennes au nord de l'Espagne et & agrave; l'ouest de la France. L'analyse de sensibilit & eacute; sugg & egrave;re que le changement climatique pourrait exposer les villes de taille moyenne au risque de transmission de dengue le plus & eacute;lev & eacute; ce r & eacute;sultat est coh & eacute;rent avec les & eacute;pid & eacute;mies de dengue r & eacute;cemment rapport & eacute;es en Europe.
