TI  - Disease Forecasting Using Climate Data and Machine Learning Techniques
PY  - 2025
AB  - Seasons contribute significantly to the prevalence of most diseases, and climate change is bound to raise their prospects. Public health planning relies heavily on climate-sensitive infectious disease modeling, which is supported by a sophisticated web of software tools. Reducing the incidence of disease outbreaks is likely feasible using predictive algorithms that can automatically determine patterns between disease and climate. Machine learning finds wide applications in healthcare for predictive analytics however, its effectiveness often depends on manual tuning and parameter modifications. This limits its full potential, especially in intricate, high-dimensional healthcare situations. Other challenges consist of complications in comparing methods, precision in forecasts, and restricted availability of diverse healthcare datasets. This study examines how temperature, humidity, and rainfall influence the prediction of diseases such as malaria, dengue, cholera, chikungunya, and Lyme disease. We used datasets titled "Climate-Driven Disease Spread", "Weather Data", "Daily Climate Time Series Data", and "Climate Insights Dataset" from Kaggle to generate the synthetic dataset. Three machine learning models Logistic Regression, Random Forest Classifier, and XGBoost Classifier were employed to predict diseases. The results showed all three models had comparable results overall, with all three achieving a weighted F1-score of 73%. Random Forest had the best macro precision of 53%, meaning the model displayed more even precision between both classes. Logistic Regression had high precision for the majority class, but did not recognize any instances in the minority class, having a recall of zero for class 1. XGBoost picked up more instances of the minority class but was still not very successful. In conclusion, Random Forest performed the best overall in a more balanced and consistent way.
