TI  - Disease Spread Prediction using Spatial Deep Convolutional Neural Network
PY  - 2025
AB  - The swift surge in infectious diseases necessitates it to predict disease spread across various regions with precision. Conventional models tend to use handcrafted features and linear assumptions, which can fail to identify intricate real-world patterns. We introduce a new Spatial Deep Convolutional Neural Network (SDCNN)-based model for predicting disease spread in this research. Our model works on multi-channel inputs from weather, population density, and past disease case data. By modelling these features as structured spatial inputs, our approach learns local and global patterns that affect disease spread. We further include an attention mechanism to dynamically concentrate on effective areas over time, enhancing the model's accuracy and robustness. We compare our model on real-world datasets of dengue and COVID-19 case histories in India. The results show that our method performs better than existing and conventional deep learning methods in effectively predicting disease spread trends, even under highly variable conditions. Notably, our method does not involve manual feature engineering and is resilient to unseen regions and patterns.
