TI  - Enhancing Dengue Forecasting with Information Theoretic Directional Features in Graph Neural Networks
PY  - 2025
AB  - Dengue fever remains a major public health threat in Thailand, where environmental, socioeconomic, and mobility factors jointly drive transmission. Traditional forecasting models such as ARIMA, GAM, and LSTM capture temporal patterns but overlook structural and directional epidemic dynamics. Recent applications of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) improve spatio-temporal forecasting, yet most rely on static adjacency matrices and environmental covariates, limiting their ability to represent interprovincial transmission pathways. To address these gaps, we introduce information-theoretic features, Delta Empowerment and Transfer Entropy Incoming, that quantify both structural influence and directional information flow among provinces. Weekly dengue case data from 77 provinces in Thailand (2017– 2019) were integrated with environmental and socioeconomic variables. Three GNN architectures STGAT, A3TGCN, and ASTGCN were evaluated using rolling cross-validation. Results show that structural and directional features markedly reduced forecasting errors compared with environmental and static baselines. STGAT achieved the largest gains, lowering MAE and RMSE by over 80%, while A3TGCN improved by 20–25% and ASTGCN by 5–10%. Beyond predictive accuracy, the framework provides structural interpretations, identifying provinces as epidemic sources, hubs, or sinks. By aligning with sustainable development goals this research demonstrates how advanced AI methods can enhance disease surveillance, support resilient health systems, and promote sustainable urban and community planning.
