TI  - Harmonizing One Health and Earth Observation to Unravel Climate Change Impact on Vector-Borne Transmitted Disease
PY  - 2025
AB  - The objective of the multidisciplinary approach presented in this paper is to combine remote sensing, climate science, ecology, and modeling to study the impact of climate change on vector-borne diseases, such as those transmitted by Aedes albopictus (Asian tiger mosquito) – dengue fever. The presence of Ae. albopictus in an area is known to be influenced by environmental factors such as temperature, precipitation, and the degree of urbanization. The “One Health” domain recognizes the interconnectedness of human, animal, and environmental health, and emphasizes the need for collaboration and integration across multiple disciplines to achieve optimal health outcomes for all three domains. Meanwhile, Earth observation data plays a crucial role in supporting One Health initiatives by providing real-time and historical data on environmental factors such as temperature, humidity, land use, and vegetation. The spread of the population depends greatly on weather conditions and ground-level wind. In this paper, we present a novel concept and processing methodology aimed at enhancing our understanding and prediction of Ae. albopictus spread by integrating multi-disciplinary knowledge into machine learning algorithms that will be trained on occurrence records of Ae. albopictus and corresponding environmental variables. This approach leverages the strengths of heterogeneous data and advanced computational techniques, improving our capacity to forecast and manage the impacts of climate change on vector-borne diseases, ultimately contributing to better public health outcomes.
