TI  - Integrating Clustering and Ensemble Machine Learning to Improve Dengue Forecasting Accuracy in Thailand
PY  - 2025
AB  - Dengue fever is the important health problem in Thailand especially in the rainy season. We analyzed multiple sources, encompassing environmental, socioeconomic, and epidemiological factors from 2017 to 2019. This study uses Hierarchical cluster to cluster the 77 provinces in Thailand into 5 clusters based on their similarities: population density, Economics and society, and disease outbreaks, and baseline model a model Random Forest (RF), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Lasso Regression (LR), and Support Vector Regression (SVR) were compared with ensemble models using Weighted Average. The paper proposes that integrating clustering with ensemble techniques, particularly RF and LSTM, significantly enhances model performance by reducing RMSE and increasing accuracy by $39.71 \pm 8.64 {\%}$ compared to non-clustered models. Additionally, ensemble methods reduce bias and variance, especially in high-risk areas like Bangkok and its metropolitan regions (Clusters 2 and 3), achieving a Test $R^{2}$ value above 0.9. These findings align with Bogado et al. (2023), which reported a $19.48 \pm 18.80 {\%}$ accuracy improvement when clustering is combined with LSTM. The study highlights the effectiveness of combining clustering and ensemble techniques for forecasting complex temporal and spatial data. These findings serve as a foundation or guideline for forecasting dengue outbreaks or other mosquito-borne diseases in the future.
