TI  - Neural Network Approaches to Predicting Vector-Borne Disease Spread with IoT Environmental Sensors
PY  - 2025
AB  - Vector-borne diseases (VBDs) are significant public health issues globally and are influenced by complex interactions between environmental variables and pathogen dynamics. This research investigates using neural network methodologies to improve the predictive modeling of VBDs dissemination, using data gathered from Internet of Things (IoT) environmental sensors. It created a robust neural network model to find trends and forecast outbreaks by merging real-time environmental data, including temperature, humidity, and rainfall, with historical disease incidence records. The model’s architecture was refined to enhance accuracy and mitigate overfitting, including dropout and batch normalization methods. Empirical findings indicate that the methodology performs traditional statistical techniques, yielding prompt forecasts that may guide public health measures and vector control efforts. It highlights the possibility of integrating machine learning algorithms with IoT technology to enable proactive management of VBDs, hence improving public health monitoring and response systems.
