TI  - Forecasting Dengue Fever in France and Thailand using XGBoost
PY  - 2022
AB  - Dengue virus infection has been classified by The World Health Organization (WHO) as a major international public health concern because of many factors such as expanding geographic distribution, increased endemic frequency, the co-circulation of multiple virus serotypes, and new emerging region of dengue hemorrhagic fever. This mosquito-borne disease is transmitted by mosquitoes of genus Aedes, such as A. albopictus and Aedes aegypti. The emerging factors are complicated and include climate factors, degree of urbanization, quality of vector control, the evolution of virus, and international travel. This research aimed to propose the model for short-term and long-term forecasting dengue fever endemics in France and Thailand using Extreme Gradient Boost Model (XGBoost). France has been selected to represent as a European country which most infections were imported from Asia, while Thailand represents Southeast Asia country where dengue fever is endemic. In addition, we used surveillance data from various data sources, the world's daily meteorological data, and related keyword queries from Google Trends as input features. We applied cross-correlation analysis to identify the time lag between surveillance data and other input features. Moreover, we experimented with various combinations of training input parameters to optimize the accuracy of the prediction model.
