TI  - TIME SERIES FORECASTING USING A SARMA MODEL FITTED ON DENGUE CASES (2010-2017): A POTENTIAL TOOL FOR AN EARLY WARNING SYSTEM IN QUEZON CITY, PHILIPPINES
PY  - 2020
JO  - SOUTHEAST ASIAN JOURNAL OF TROPICAL MEDICINE AND PUBLIC HEALTH
AB  - Public health surveillance is one of the key strategies being prescribed by the World Health Organization (WHO) for dengue prevention and control programs. Information generated from dengue surveillance are useful in the timely identification of outbreaks and epidemics to initiate prompt response. Time series modelling can be used in establishing an early warning system that can forecast a potential dengue outbreak. This study was done to assess the potential of a time series model in dengue forecasting. Using Box-Jenkins method, time series modeling was performed on the dengue surveillance data of the Quezon City Epidemiology and Surveillance Unit. A seasonal ARMA model that is fitted with the actual dengue cases from 2010 to 2016 was used in forecasting monthly dengue cases in 2017. Monthly time series data from 2010 to 2016 observed inherent seasonality and stationarity. The model that demonstrated the best fit was ARMA(0,3)x(2,2)12, which can perform a static forecast with a mean absolute percentage error of 25.19%. The SARMA model proved to be a potential tool for an early warning system, which will be helpful for local government officials, disease surveillance professionals, and program managers in public health decision making and in implementing timely and responsive actions.
