TI  - Citywide District-Level Weekly Dengue Forecasting: Evaluation of Bayesian Spatiotemporal Models for Routine Surveillance
PY  - 2025
AB  - The local government of Quezon City performs weekly barangay-level dengue case reporting as part of its routine surveillance. This activity provides an opportunity to improve dengue surveillance through modeling and forecasting. Models trained on long-term data, similar to the first study, showed that integrating sociodemographic variables with weather data improved predictive accuracy, as evidenced by reductions in DIC and WAIC scores and enhanced LogCPO values [8]. This study extends the work by presenting three Bayesian spatiotemporal models which incorporates: weather (Model 1), weather and socio-demographic data (Model 2), with interventions (Model 3) using R-INLA with the objective of predicting the risk of dengue at district level on a weekly basis. All models were trained on weekly data from January 2023–December 2023, with predictor lags (4–8 weeks) and evaluated by cross-validation. Model 3 achieved the lowest MAE (5.79 vs. 5.99/5.80 for Models 1/2, p < 0.05) and best fit (WAIC: 1163.8 vs. 1169.0/1165.3), with computational costs scaling modestly (runtime: 2.97 seconds; memory: 25.2 MB). The results showed that even complex models are computationally feasible for routine surveillance, thereby enabling district-level risk maps to guide outbreak response. Model 3 is recommended for integration into city district-level disease surveillance platforms.
