TI  - An Anonymized Geospatial Dengue Surveillance Dataset for Risk Stratification: A Municipality-Year Analytical Resource for Unsupervised Clustering
PY  - 2026
JO  - Data
AB  - Dengue fever poses an ongoing challenge to global and Colombian public health. Although surveillance microdata are widely available, there remains a gap in converting them into actionable epidemiological intelligence. This study consolidates 15 years (2010-2024) of anonymized records from Colombia's National Public Health Surveillance System, encompassing 303,801 cases across 197 municipalities. Microdata were aggregated into municipality-year units using seven indicators covering magnitude, severity, demographics and surveillance performance. K-means clustering validation via Elbow and Silhouette methods identified four epidemiological profiles: high-transmission urban settings, dispersed rural risk areas, pediatric-predominant territories, and high-severity clusters with elevated hospitalization and case fatality rates.
